  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Palantir Usg recorded $1.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date across 49 awards, with an average award size of $23.7M. Obligations are up 56.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $1.1B in FY2026 to date. Custom Computer Programming Services is the largest market by obligation at $1B, followed by Software Publishers at $124M. Fiscal-year obligations rose from $67.6M in FY21 to $198M in FY22, then were $151M in FY23, $288M in FY24, and $745M in FY25 before reaching $1.2B in FY26. The Department of State, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, and the U.S. International Development Finance Corporation recorded obligations of $18M, $12.1M, and $8.1M respectively in FY2026 to date. Other recorded markets include Other Management Consulting Services at $22M and research-related work at $247K and $236K. Current-year totals are partial and contract obligations are the basis for these figures unless otherwise noted for award-value tables.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Palantir Usg's FY2026 obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for the bulk of FY2026 obligations, with $1.1B. Custom Computer Programming Services drives most industry obligations at $1B. Non-DOD agencies each account for substantially smaller FY2026 obligations, such as the Department of State at $18M and NASA at $12.1M. Fiscal-year data show growth in obligations into FY26 after variability in prior years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and award patterns appear in the FY2026 data?",
        "body": "Custom Computer Programming Services and Software Publishers dominate market obligations, at $1B and $124M respectively. Palantir Usg logged 49 awards in FY2026 to date with an average award size of $23.7M. Other recorded markets include Other Management Consulting Services at $22M and research-related NAICS entries at $247K and $236K. FY2026 totals are partial and reflect obligations rather than total contract values unless noted."
      }
    ]
